    "case_summary": "An 80-year-old woman with a history of hypertension, gout, and osteoarthritis presented with several days of nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. She had a productive cough and dyspnea on exertion for the past two weeks, along with progressively worsening fatigue and severe malaise. No new medications were started in the past several weeks. Additional history revealed poor oral intake for several weeks and consumption of 3-4 glasses of wine daily. The patient had sustained a dog bite on her right thumb approximately one week before admission.\n\nOn presentation, patient was:- Afebrile- Tachycardic (122 beats/min)- Hypotensive (95/55 mm Hg)- Well-appearing and in no acute distress- Clear lungs on auscultation- Soft and nontender abdomen- Nonblanchable, petechial rash over bilateral lower extremities to the level of the knees\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Complete Blood Count:  * Leukocytosis: 18.4 × 10⁹/L (93% granulocytes)  * Thrombocytopenia: Initially 99 × 10⁹/L, reaching nadir of 9 × 10⁹/L  * Previous platelet count (3 months prior): 503 × 10⁹/L- Blood Work:  * Macrocytosis  * Low iron  * Low transferrin percent saturation  * Elevated ferritin  * Normal haptoglobin, fibrinogen, vitamin B12, folate, and copper levels- Peripheral blood smear: Showed thrombocytopenia without platelet clumping, left shift with reactive neutrophils, no schistocytes or blasts- Blood cultures: Gram stain showed thin, fusiform gram-negative rods in two of four bottlesImaging Studies:- CT abdomen and pelvis: Normal- CT angiogram chest: Showed smoking-related changes and centrilobular micronodulesAdditional Tests (all negative):- SARS-CoV-2- Respiratory syncytial virus- Influenza- Urine Streptococcus pneumoniae antigen- Urine Legionella pneumophila antigen- HIV- Hepatitis B and C virus serologies",
